22:54:40 out   # Cue Player
22:54:40 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
22:54:40 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
22:54:40 out   and can start and stop Audacity's transport over Audacity's scripting pipe.
22:54:40 out   The layout is deliberately plain: a large picture, a transport with a large
22:54:40 out   clock, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
22:54:40 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
22:54:40 out   then press Play.
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
22:54:40 out   │ CUE PLAYER video for takes sample.mp4 │
22:54:40 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
22:54:40 out   │ │
22:54:40 out   │ video picture │
22:54:40 out   │ │
22:54:40 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
22:54:40 out   │ ▶ ■ 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 AUDACITY TRANSPORT ● │
22:54:40 out   │ ────────────────●───────────────▲────────────── │
22:54:40 out   │ START AT [00:00:04] -10s -1s +1s +10s [Use playhead] │
22:54:40 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   ## What it does
22:54:40 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
22:54:40 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
22:54:40 out   - **Typed start cue.** Enter `HH:MM:SS` (or `MM:SS`, or seconds) and press Enter.
22:54:40 out   The cue is where Stop rewinds to and where Play restarts after the end.
22:54:40 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
22:54:40 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
22:54:40 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
22:54:40 out   - **Audacity transport.** Play sends `Play:` and Stop sends `Stop:` to
22:54:40 out   Audacity's `mod-script-pipe`. The link can be switched off without stopping
22:54:40 out   the video.
22:54:40 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
22:54:40 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
22:54:40 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
22:54:40 out   ## Build and run
22:54:40 out   Requirements for building:
22:54:40 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
22:54:40 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
22:54:40 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
22:54:40 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
22:54:40 out   AppImage script
22:54:40 out   From this folder:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   cargo build --release
22:54:40 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   Open a file directly:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   Debug build and tests:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   cargo run
22:54:40 out   cargo build
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
22:54:40 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
22:54:40 out   ## Controls
22:54:40 out   | Input | Action |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Space | Play / Stop |
22:54:40 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the start cue |
22:54:40 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
22:54:40 out   | Home | Jump to the start cue |
22:54:40 out   | O | Open the file browser |
22:54:40 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
22:54:40 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
22:54:40 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
22:54:40 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
22:54:40 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
22:54:40 out   The `-10s`, `-1s`, `+1s`, `+10s` buttons nudge the cue. **Use playhead** copies
22:54:40 out   the current scrub position into the cue; this is the quickest way to pick a
22:54:40 out   start frame by eye.
22:54:40 out   ## Connecting Audacity
22:54:40 out   1. In Audacity, open **Tools > Modules** (older versions: **Edit > Preferences >
22:54:40 out   Modules**) and enable **mod-script-pipe**.
22:54:40 out   2. Restart Audacity. A pair of named pipes appears, normally
22:54:40 out   `/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.to.<uid>` and
22:54:40 out   `/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.from.<uid>`.
22:54:40 out   3. Start Cue Player. The chip should read **linked**. If it reads
22:54:40 out   **pipe not found**, check the module and restart Audacity.
22:54:40 out   4. Switch **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** off if you only want picture playback.
22:54:40 out   The app sends `Play:` and `Stop:`; it does not seek Audacity. Set the Audacity
22:54:40 out   play head to the matching time yourself. If your Audacity build uses different
22:54:40 out   commands, set `CUE_AUDACITY_PLAY` and `CUE_AUDACITY_STOP`. A custom pipe path can
22:54:40 out   be given with `CUE_AUDACITY_PIPE`.
22:54:40 out   > Tested live against Audacity 3.7.7. Audacity 4.0 was not installed on the
22:54:40 out   > build machine; the same `mod-script-pipe` commands are expected to work.
22:54:40 out   ## AppImage
22:54:40 out   Build the AppImage with:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
22:54:40 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   Run it with:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   chmod +x dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
22:54:40 out   ./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
22:54:40 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
22:54:40 out   `./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
22:54:40 out   ## Verification flags
22:54:40 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
22:54:40 out   ```sh
22:54:40 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
22:54:40 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
22:54:40 out   # Exercise playback, the typed cue and the painted HUD, then write a JSON report.
22:54:40 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
22:54:40 out   ```
22:54:40 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
22:54:40 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
22:54:40 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
22:54:40 out   ## Project layout
22:54:40 out   | Path | Purpose |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, layout, drawing, input, screenshot/self-test |
22:54:40 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, seek and preview |
22:54:40 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
22:54:40 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | pipe discovery and command sending |
22:54:40 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, fonts, shapes, time parsing/formatting |
22:54:40 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
22:54:40 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
22:54:40 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, cueing and sync |
22:54:40 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
22:54:40 out   ## Known limits
22:54:40 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
22:54:40 out   the typed cue is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
22:54:40 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
22:54:40 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
22:54:40 out   - Audacity 4.0's pipe was not available on this machine; only 3.7.7 was
22:54:40 out   verified. Commands are configurable for that reason.
22:54:40 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
22:54:40 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
22:54:40 out   ## Licence
22:54:40 out   Cue Player is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain
22:54:40 out   under the Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
22:54:40 out   --- MECHANICS
22:54:40 out   # Cue Player mechanics
22:54:40 out   This sheet lists the values that shape how Cue Player behaves. There is no
22:54:40 out   original program to compare against for this build, so every value below is
22:54:40 out   **estimated** from the request and from how Audacity's scripting pipe actually
22:54:40 out   behaves on the development machine. The same values live in
22:54:40 out   [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
22:54:40 out   Cue Player is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means playback,
22:54:40 out   cueing, the Audacity hand-off and the interface timings that affect them.
22:54:40 out   ## Playback
22:54:40 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Playback rate | `1.0x` (the play head advances by the frame delta) | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Output frame rate | `30 fps` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Maximum decoded width | `1280 px` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Maximum decoded height | `720 px` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Decoded frame queue | `12 frames` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Decoder channel buffer | `8 frames` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Longest accepted frame step | `0.1 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   Frames are decoded by `ffmpeg` to raw RGBA, scaled with bicubic filtering to fit
22:54:40 out   inside 1280x720 while preserving aspect ratio, and sampled at a fixed 30 fps.
22:54:40 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
22:54:40 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing playback. The image is letterboxed in the
22:54:40 out   window; it is never stretched.
22:54:40 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
22:54:40 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
22:54:40 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
22:54:40 out   ## Start cue
22:54:40 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Default start cue | `0 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Cue nudge | `1 s` (`-1s` / `+1s`) | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Large cue nudge | `10 s` (`-10s` / `+10s`) | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Arrow seek step | `5 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Cue input format | `hours:minutes:seconds` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   The start field accepts `HH:MM:SS`, `MM:SS`, or plain seconds and also accepts
22:54:40 out   fractional seconds (`00:01:02.500`). The value is clamped to `0..duration`.
22:54:40 out   Pressing Enter or clicking away commits it; Escape restores the previous cue.
22:54:40 out   The same cue is used in three places:
22:54:40 out   1. **Stop** rewinds the picture to the cue, so the next take starts from the same
22:54:40 out   frame.
22:54:40 out   2. **Play** after the clip has ended restarts from the cue.
22:54:40 out   3. The cue is drawn on the scrub bar as a bright marker, separate from the play
22:54:40 out   head.
22:54:40 out   The scrub bar is only a preview tool. Dragging it moves the picture and clock,
22:54:40 out   but it does not change the typed start cue. Use **Use playhead** to copy the
22:54:40 out   current scrub position into the cue.
22:54:40 out   ## Audacity hand-off
22:54:40 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Play command | `Play:` | estimated, verified against Audacity 3.7.7 |
22:54:40 out   | Stop command | `Stop:` | estimated, verified against Audacity 3.7.7 |
22:54:40 out   | Sync enabled by default | `true` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Pipe poll interval | `2.0 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   When **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** is on, pressing Play sends `Play:` and pressing Stop
22:54:40 out   sends `Stop:` to Audacity's `mod-script-pipe` named pipe. There is deliberately
22:54:40 out   no timeline synchronization: line up the start cue in Cue Player with the
22:54:40 out   Audacity play head by hand, then start from either side. Stop is sent even when
22:54:40 out   the video had already reached its end, so the audio transport cannot keep
22:54:40 out   running after the picture stops.
22:54:40 out   The pipe is discovered in `/tmp`, `$TMPDIR` and `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` as
22:54:40 out   `audacity_script_pipe.to.<uid>` (or `<user>`). It is re-checked every 2 seconds.
22:54:40 out   Override the location with `CUE_AUDACITY_PIPE`, or the commands with
22:54:40 out   `CUE_AUDACITY_PLAY` and `CUE_AUDACITY_STOP`. Pipe errors are shown once per
22:54:40 out   session as a toast.
22:54:40 out   ## Controls and interface
22:54:40 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Window size | `1180x760` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Toast duration | `4.5 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | File browser double-click window | `0.38 s` | estimated |
22:54:40 out   | Input | Action |
22:54:40 out   | --- | --- |
22:54:40 out   | Space | Play / Stop (Stop returns to the cue) |
22:54:40 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the cue |
22:54:40 out   | Enter (in start field) | Apply the typed start cue |
22:54:40 out   | Escape (in start field) | Discard the edit |
22:54:40 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
22:54:40 out   | Home | Jump to the cue |
22:54:40 out   | O | Open the file browser |
22:54:40 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
22:54:40 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a position, keep playing/paused state |
22:54:40 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
22:54:40 out   The layout switches to a compact transport below 820 px wide: the clock is
22:54:40 out   smaller, the Audacity chip is narrower and the cue buttons are tighter, so
22:54:40 out   nothing runs outside the panel.
22:54:40 out   ## Sources and unknowns
22:54:40 out   - The request defines the behaviour: Play/Stop control Audacity, typed start
22:54:40 out   point, video only, broad format support, AppImage.
22:54:40 out   - Audacity 3.7.7 on this machine accepts `Play:` and `Stop:` and rejects the
22:54:40 out   combined `PlayStop:` command. Audacity 4.0 was not available to test; its
22:54:40 out   `mod-script-pipe` command vocabulary is assumed to keep `Play:` and `Stop:`.
22:54:40 out   - Every numeric value is a design choice for this build, not a copied constant.
22:54:40 out   - Decode size and frame rate trade sharpness for CPU and memory; they can be
22:54:40 out   changed in `src/player.rs` and kept in step with `mechanics.json`.
