23:00:17 out   # Cue Player
23:00:17 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
23:00:17 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
23:00:17 out   and can start and stop Audacity 4.0's transport by sending it the Space key.
23:00:17 out   The layout is deliberately plain: a large picture, a transport with a large
23:00:17 out   clock, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
23:00:17 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
23:00:17 out   then press Play.
23:00:17 out   ```
23:00:17 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
23:00:17 out   │ CUE PLAYER video for takes sample.mp4 │
23:00:17 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
23:00:17 out   │ │
23:00:17 out   │ video picture │
23:00:17 out   │ │
23:00:17 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
23:00:17 out   │ ▶ ■ 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 AUDACITY TRANSPORT ● │
23:00:17 out   │ ────────────────●───────────────▲────────────── │
23:00:17 out   │ START AT [00:00:04] -10s -1s +1s +10s [Use playhead] │
23:00:17 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
23:00:17 out   ```
23:00:17 out   ## What it does
23:00:17 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
23:00:17 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
23:00:17 out   - **Typed start cue.** Enter `HH:MM:SS` (or `MM:SS`, or seconds) and press Enter.
23:00:17 out   The cue is where Stop rewinds to and where Play restarts after the end.
23:00:17 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
23:00:17 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
23:00:17 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
23:00:17 out   - **Audacity 4.0 transport.** Play and Stop both send a Space key press to the
23:00:17 out   Audacity 4.0 window, which is Audacity's default Play/Stop shortcut. The link
23:00:17 out   can be switched off without stopping the video.
23:00:17 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
23:00:17 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
23:00:17 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
23:00:17 out   ## Build and run
23:00:17 out   Requirements for building:
23:00:17 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
23:00:17 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
23:00:17 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
23:00:17 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
23:00:17 out   AppImage script
23:00:17 out   From this folder:
23:00:17 out   ```sh
23:00:17 out   cargo build --release
23:00:17 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
23:00:17 out   ```
23:00:17 out   Open a file directly:
23:00:17 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
23:00:17 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
23:00:17 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
23:00:17 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
23:00:17 out   The `-10s`, `-1s`, `+1s`, `+10s` buttons nudge the cue. **Use playhead** copies
23:00:17 out   the current scrub position into the cue; this is the quickest way to pick a
23:00:17 out   start frame by eye.
23:00:17 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
23:00:17 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its main window open.
23:00:17 out   2. Start Cue Player. The chip should read **keyboard ready**. If it reads
23:00:17 out   **Audacity not found**, start Audacity first, then press Play again.
23:00:17 out   3. Switch **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** off if you only want picture playback.
23:00:17 out   Cue Player finds the Audacity window, gives it focus for a moment, taps Space
23:00:17 out   (the default Play/Stop shortcut in Audacity 4.0), then returns focus to the
23:00:17 out   video player. It does not seek Audacity; set the Audacity play head to the
23:00:17 out   matching time yourself.
23:00:17 out   > This uses the X11 keyboard API. It was tested live against Audacity 4.0.1 on
23:00:17 out   > an X11 desktop. A native Wayland session is not supported.
23:00:17 out   ## AppImage
23:00:17 out   Build the AppImage with:
23:00:17 out   ```sh
23:00:17 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
23:00:17 out   ```
23:00:17 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
23:00:17 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
23:00:17 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, cueing and sync |
23:00:17 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
23:00:17 out   ## Known limits
23:00:17 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
23:00:17 out   the typed cue is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
23:00:17 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
23:00:17 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
23:00:17 out   - Audacity 4.0's transport is driven by its Space shortcut, so Audacity must be
23:00:17 out   running on an X11 desktop and its main window must be open. Native Wayland is
23:00:17 out   not supported.
23:00:17 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
23:00:17 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
23:00:17 out   ## Licence
23:00:17 out   Cue Player is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain
23:00:17 out   under the Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
