23:26:53 out   [package]
23:26:53 out   name = "cue-video-player"
23:26:53 out   version = "1.0.0"
23:26:53 out   edition = "2021"
23:26:53 out   description = "A small cue-based video player for audition and foley work"
23:26:53 out   license = "MIT"
23:26:53 out   [dependencies]
23:26:53 out   macroquad = "0.4.16"
23:26:53 out   serde_json = "1"
23:26:53 out   [profile.release]
23:26:53 out   opt-level = 3
23:26:53 out   lto = true
23:26:53 out   codegen-units = 1
23:26:53 out   strip = true
23:26:53 out   # Cue Player
23:26:53 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
23:26:53 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
23:26:53 out   and can start and stop Audacity 4.0's transport by sending it the Space key.
23:26:53 out   The layout is deliberately plain: a large picture, a transport with a large
23:26:53 out   clock, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
23:26:53 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
23:26:53 out   then press Play.
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
23:26:53 out   │ CUE PLAYER video for takes sample.mp4 │
23:26:53 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
23:26:53 out   │ │
23:26:53 out   │ video picture │
23:26:53 out   │ │
23:26:53 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
23:26:53 out   │ ▶ ■ 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 AUDACITY TRANSPORT ● │
23:26:53 out   │ ────────────────●───────────────▲────────────── │
23:26:53 out   │ START AT [00:00:04] -10s -1s +1s +10s [Use playhead] │
23:26:53 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   ## What it does
23:26:53 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
23:26:53 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
23:26:53 out   - **Typed start cue.** Enter `HH:MM:SS` (or `MM:SS`, or seconds) and press Enter.
23:26:53 out   The cue is where Stop rewinds to and where Play restarts after the end.
23:26:53 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
23:26:53 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
23:26:53 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
23:26:53 out   - **Audacity 4.0 transport.** Play and Stop both send a Space key press to the
23:26:53 out   Audacity 4.0 window, which is Audacity's default Play/Stop shortcut. The link
23:26:53 out   can be switched off without stopping the video.
23:26:53 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
23:26:53 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
23:26:53 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
23:26:53 out   ## Build and run
23:26:53 out   Requirements for building:
23:26:53 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
23:26:53 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
23:26:53 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
23:26:53 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
23:26:53 out   AppImage script
23:26:53 out   From this folder:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   cargo build --release
23:26:53 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   Open a file directly:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   Debug build and tests:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   cargo run
23:26:53 out   cargo build
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
23:26:53 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
23:26:53 out   ## Controls
23:26:53 out   | Input | Action |
23:26:53 out   | --- | --- |
23:26:53 out   | Space | Play / Stop |
23:26:53 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the start cue |
23:26:53 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
23:26:53 out   | Home | Jump to the start cue |
23:26:53 out   | O | Open the file browser |
23:26:53 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
23:26:53 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
23:26:53 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
23:26:53 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
23:26:53 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
23:26:53 out   The `-10s`, `-1s`, `+1s`, `+10s` buttons nudge the cue. **Use playhead** copies
23:26:53 out   the current scrub position into the cue; this is the quickest way to pick a
23:26:53 out   start frame by eye.
23:26:53 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
23:26:53 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its main window open.
23:26:53 out   2. Start Cue Player. The chip should read **keyboard ready**. If it reads
23:26:53 out   **Audacity not found**, start Audacity first, then press Play again.
23:26:53 out   3. Switch **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** off if you only want picture playback.
23:26:53 out   Cue Player finds the Audacity window, gives it focus for a moment, taps Space
23:26:53 out   (the default Play/Stop shortcut in Audacity 4.0), then returns focus to the
23:26:53 out   video player. It does not seek Audacity; set the Audacity play head to the
23:26:53 out   matching time yourself.
23:26:53 out   > This uses the X11 keyboard API. It was tested live against Audacity 4.0.1 on
23:26:53 out   > an X11 desktop. A native Wayland session is not supported.
23:26:53 out   ## AppImage
23:26:53 out   Build the AppImage with:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
23:26:53 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   Run it with:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   chmod +x dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
23:26:53 out   ./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
23:26:53 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
23:26:53 out   `./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
23:26:53 out   ## Verification flags
23:26:53 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
23:26:53 out   ```sh
23:26:53 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
23:26:53 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
23:26:53 out   # Exercise playback, the typed cue and the painted HUD, then write a JSON report.
23:26:53 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
23:26:53 out   ```
23:26:53 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
23:26:53 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
23:26:53 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
23:26:53 out   ## Project layout
23:26:53 out   | Path | Purpose |
23:26:53 out   | --- | --- |
23:26:53 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, layout, drawing, input, screenshot/self-test |
23:26:53 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, seek and preview |
23:26:53 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
23:26:53 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | Audacity window discovery and Space-key sending |
23:26:53 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, fonts, shapes, time parsing/formatting |
23:26:53 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
23:26:53 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
23:26:53 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, cueing and sync |
23:26:53 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
23:26:53 out   ## Known limits
23:26:53 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
23:26:53 out   the typed cue is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
23:26:53 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
23:26:53 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
23:26:53 out   - Audacity 4.0's transport is driven by its Space shortcut, so Audacity must be
23:26:53 out   running on an X11 desktop and its main window must be open. Native Wayland is
23:26:53 out   not supported.
23:26:53 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
23:26:53 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
23:26:53 out   ## Licence
23:26:53 out   Cue Player is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain
23:26:53 out   under the Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
