00:40:52 out   === README ===
00:40:52 out   # Cue Player
00:40:52 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
00:40:52 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
00:40:52 out   and can start and stop Audacity 4.0's transport by sending it the Space key.
00:40:52 out   The layout is deliberately plain: a large picture, a transport with a large
00:40:52 out   clock, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
00:40:52 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
00:40:52 out   then press Play.
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
00:40:52 out   │ CUE PLAYER video for takes sample.mp4 │
00:40:52 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
00:40:52 out   │ │
00:40:52 out   │ video picture │
00:40:52 out   │ │
00:40:52 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
00:40:52 out   │ ▶ ■ 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 AUDACITY TRANSPORT ● │
00:40:52 out   │ ────────────────●───────────────▲────────────── │
00:40:52 out   │ START AT [00:00:04] -10s -1s +1s +10s [Use playhead] │
00:40:52 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   ## What it does
00:40:52 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
00:40:52 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
00:40:52 out   - **Typed start cue.** Enter `HH:MM:SS` (or `MM:SS`, or seconds) and press Enter.
00:40:52 out   The cue is where Stop rewinds to and where Play restarts after the end.
00:40:52 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
00:40:52 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
00:40:52 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
00:40:52 out   - **Audacity 4.0 transport.** Play and Stop both send a Space key press to the
00:40:52 out   Audacity 4.0 window, which is Audacity's default Play/Stop shortcut. The link
00:40:52 out   can be switched off without stopping the video.
00:40:52 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
00:40:52 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
00:40:52 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
00:40:52 out   ## Build and run
00:40:52 out   Requirements for building:
00:40:52 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
00:40:52 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
00:40:52 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
00:40:52 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
00:40:52 out   AppImage script
00:40:52 out   From this folder:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   cargo build --release
00:40:52 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   Open a file directly:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   Debug build and tests:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   cargo run
00:40:52 out   cargo build
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
00:40:52 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
00:40:52 out   ## Controls
00:40:52 out   | Input | Action |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Space | Play / Stop |
00:40:52 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the start cue |
00:40:52 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
00:40:52 out   | Home | Jump to the start cue |
00:40:52 out   | O | Open the file browser |
00:40:52 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
00:40:52 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
00:40:52 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
00:40:52 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
00:40:52 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
00:40:52 out   The `-10s`, `-1s`, `+1s`, `+10s` buttons nudge the cue. **Use playhead** copies
00:40:52 out   the current scrub position into the cue; this is the quickest way to pick a
00:40:52 out   start frame by eye.
00:40:52 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
00:40:52 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its main window open.
00:40:52 out   2. Start Cue Player. The chip should read **keyboard ready**. If it reads
00:40:52 out   **Audacity not found**, start Audacity first, then press Play again.
00:40:52 out   3. Switch **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** off if you only want picture playback.
00:40:52 out   Cue Player finds the Audacity window, gives it focus for a moment, taps Space
00:40:52 out   (the default Play/Stop shortcut in Audacity 4.0), then returns focus to the
00:40:52 out   video player. It does not seek Audacity; set the Audacity play head to the
00:40:52 out   matching time yourself.
00:40:52 out   > This uses the X11 keyboard API. It was tested live against Audacity 4.0.1 on
00:40:52 out   > an X11 desktop. A native Wayland session is not supported.
00:40:52 out   ## AppImage
00:40:52 out   Build the AppImage with:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
00:40:52 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   Run it with:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   chmod +x dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
00:40:52 out   ./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
00:40:52 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
00:40:52 out   `./dist/CuePlayer-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
00:40:52 out   ## Verification flags
00:40:52 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
00:40:52 out   ```sh
00:40:52 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
00:40:52 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
00:40:52 out   # Exercise playback, the typed cue and the painted HUD, then write a JSON report.
00:40:52 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
00:40:52 out   ```
00:40:52 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
00:40:52 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
00:40:52 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
00:40:52 out   ## Project layout
00:40:52 out   | Path | Purpose |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, layout, drawing, input, screenshot/self-test |
00:40:52 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, seek and preview |
00:40:52 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
00:40:52 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | Audacity window discovery and Space-key sending |
00:40:52 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, fonts, shapes, time parsing/formatting |
00:40:52 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
00:40:52 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
00:40:52 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, cueing and sync |
00:40:52 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
00:40:52 out   ## Known limits
00:40:52 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
00:40:52 out   the typed cue is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
00:40:52 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
00:40:52 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
00:40:52 out   - Audacity 4.0's transport is driven by its Space shortcut, so Audacity must be
00:40:52 out   running on an X11 desktop and its main window must be open. Native Wayland is
00:40:52 out   not supported.
00:40:52 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
00:40:52 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
00:40:52 out   ## Licence
00:40:52 out   Cue Player is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain
00:40:52 out   under the Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
00:40:52 out   === MECHANICS ===
00:40:52 out   # Cue Player mechanics
00:40:52 out   This sheet lists the values that shape how Cue Player behaves. There is no
00:40:52 out   original program to compare against for this build, so every value below is
00:40:52 out   **estimated** from the request and from how Audacity 4.0's Space shortcut
00:40:52 out   behaves on the development machine. The same values live in
00:40:52 out   [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
00:40:52 out   Cue Player is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means playback,
00:40:52 out   cueing, the Audacity hand-off and the interface timings that affect them.
00:40:52 out   ## Playback
00:40:52 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Playback rate | `1.0x` (the play head advances by the frame delta) | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Output frame rate | `30 fps` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Maximum decoded width | `1280 px` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Maximum decoded height | `720 px` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Decoded frame queue | `12 frames` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Decoder channel buffer | `8 frames` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Longest accepted frame step | `0.1 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   Frames are decoded by `ffmpeg` to raw RGBA, scaled with bicubic filtering to fit
00:40:52 out   inside 1280x720 while preserving aspect ratio, and sampled at a fixed 30 fps.
00:40:52 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
00:40:52 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing playback. The image is letterboxed in the
00:40:52 out   window; it is never stretched.
00:40:52 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
00:40:52 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
00:40:52 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
00:40:52 out   ## Start cue
00:40:52 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Default start cue | `0 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Cue nudge | `1 s` (`-1s` / `+1s`) | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Large cue nudge | `10 s` (`-10s` / `+10s`) | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Arrow seek step | `5 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Cue input format | `hours:minutes:seconds` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   The start field accepts `HH:MM:SS`, `MM:SS`, or plain seconds and also accepts
00:40:52 out   fractional seconds (`00:01:02.500`). The value is clamped to `0..duration`.
00:40:52 out   Pressing Enter or clicking away commits it; Escape restores the previous cue.
00:40:52 out   The same cue is used in three places:
00:40:52 out   1. **Stop** rewinds the picture to the cue, so the next take starts from the same
00:40:52 out   frame.
00:40:52 out   2. **Play** after the clip has ended restarts from the cue.
00:40:52 out   3. The cue is drawn on the scrub bar as a bright marker, separate from the play
00:40:52 out   head.
00:40:52 out   The scrub bar is only a preview tool. Dragging it moves the picture and clock,
00:40:52 out   but it does not change the typed start cue. Use **Use playhead** to copy the
00:40:52 out   current scrub position into the cue.
00:40:52 out   ## Audacity hand-off
00:40:52 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Play key | `Space` | verified against Audacity 4.0.1 |
00:40:52 out   | Stop key | `Space` | verified against Audacity 4.0.1 |
00:40:52 out   | Sync enabled by default | `true` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Audacity window poll interval | `2.0 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   When **AUDACITY TRANSPORT** is on, pressing Play or Stop finds the Audacity 4.0
00:40:52 out   window, focuses it for a moment, taps Space (Audacity's default Play/Stop
00:40:52 out   shortcut) through XTest, then returns focus to Cue Player. There is deliberately
00:40:52 out   no timeline synchronization: line up the start cue in Cue Player with the
00:40:52 out   Audacity play head by hand, then start from either side. Stop is sent even when
00:40:52 out   the video had already reached its end, so the audio transport cannot keep
00:40:52 out   running after the picture stops.
00:40:52 out   The Audacity window is re-checked every 2 seconds and the chip reads **keyboard
00:40:52 out   ready** when it is found. If Audacity is not running, or the X11 keyboard
00:40:52 out   libraries are unavailable, an error is shown once per session as a toast. The
00:40:52 out   hand-off uses X11, so a native Wayland session is not supported.
00:40:52 out   ## Controls and interface
00:40:52 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Window size | `1180x760` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Toast duration | `4.5 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | File browser double-click window | `0.38 s` | estimated |
00:40:52 out   | Input | Action |
00:40:52 out   | --- | --- |
00:40:52 out   | Space | Play / Stop (Stop returns to the cue) |
00:40:52 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the cue |
00:40:52 out   | Enter (in start field) | Apply the typed start cue |
00:40:52 out   | Escape (in start field) | Discard the edit |
00:40:52 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
00:40:52 out   | Home | Jump to the cue |
00:40:52 out   | O | Open the file browser |
00:40:52 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
00:40:52 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a position, keep playing/paused state |
00:40:52 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
00:40:52 out   The layout switches to a compact transport below 820 px wide: the clock is
00:40:52 out   smaller, the Audacity chip is narrower and the cue buttons are tighter, so
00:40:52 out   nothing runs outside the panel.
00:40:52 out   ## Sources and unknowns
00:40:52 out   - The request defines the behaviour: Play/Stop control Audacity, typed start
00:40:52 out   point, video only, broad format support, AppImage.
00:40:52 out   - Audacity 4.0.1 on this machine responds to an injected Space key with its
00:40:52 out   `playback/toggle-play-stop` action. The hand-off uses X11/XTest, so native
00:40:52 out   Wayland is not covered.
00:40:52 out   - Every numeric value is a design choice for this build, not a copied constant.
00:40:52 out   - Decode size and frame rate trade sharpness for CPU and memory; they can be
00:40:52 out   changed in `src/player.rs` and kept in step with `mechanics.json`.
