02:38:00 out   # VidCue
02:38:00 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
02:38:00 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
02:38:00 out   and starts and stops Audacity 4.0's transport by sending it the Space key.
02:38:00 out   After the key is sent, VidCue brings itself back to the front.
02:38:00 out   The layout is deliberately plain and compact: a large picture, a short transport
02:38:00 out   strip, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
02:38:00 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
02:38:00 out   then press Play. The window can be shrunk to a small strip without the controls
02:38:00 out   spilling outside the panel.
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
02:38:00 out   │ ▶ VIDCUE video for takes sample.mp4 ⚙ │
02:38:00 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
02:38:00 out   │ │
02:38:00 out   │ video picture │
02:38:00 out   │ │
02:38:00 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
02:38:00 out   │ ▶ ■ 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 AUDACITY [ on ] │
02:38:00 out   │ ────────────────●───────────────▲────────────── │
02:38:00 out   │ START AT [00:00:04.000] -10s -1s +1s +10s [Use playhead] │
02:38:00 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   ## What it does
02:38:00 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
02:38:00 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
02:38:00 out   - **Typed start cue with milliseconds.** Enter `HH:MM:SS.mmm` (for example
02:38:00 out   `00:01:23.500`), `MM:SS`, or plain seconds and press Enter. The cue is where
02:38:00 out   Stop rewinds to and where Play restarts after the end.
02:38:00 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
02:38:00 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
02:38:00 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
02:38:00 out   - **Audacity 4.0 transport.** Play and Stop both send a Space key press to the
02:38:00 out   Audacity 4.0 window, which is Audacity's default Play/Stop shortcut. VidCue
02:38:00 out   then raises and focuses its own window again. The link can be switched off
02:38:00 out   without stopping the video.
02:38:00 out   - **Compact controls.** The transport collapses to two short rows, with small
02:38:00 out   play/stop buttons, a compact clock, trimmed nudge buttons and a narrower
02:38:00 out   Audacity chip as the window gets smaller.
02:38:00 out   - **Accent colour setting.** The gear at the top right opens a small settings
02:38:00 out   panel with six choices: orange, yellow, green, blue, purple and red. Purple
02:38:00 out   is the default, matching the app icon.
02:38:00 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
02:38:00 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
02:38:00 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
02:38:00 out   ## Build and run
02:38:00 out   Requirements for building:
02:38:00 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
02:38:00 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
02:38:00 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
02:38:00 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
02:38:00 out   AppImage script
02:38:00 out   From this folder:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   cargo build --release
02:38:00 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   Open a file directly:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   Debug build and tests:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   cargo run
02:38:00 out   cargo build
02:38:00 out   cargo test
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
02:38:00 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
02:38:00 out   ## Controls
02:38:00 out   | Input | Action |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Space | Play / Stop |
02:38:00 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the start cue |
02:38:00 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
02:38:00 out   | Home | Jump to the start cue |
02:38:00 out   | O | Open the file browser |
02:38:00 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
02:38:00 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
02:38:00 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
02:38:00 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
02:38:00 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
02:38:00 out   The `-10s`, `-1s`, `+1s`, `+10s` buttons nudge the cue. In the narrowest layout
02:38:00 out   the two large nudges make way for the start field and the single-second nudges.
02:38:00 out   **Use playhead** copies the current scrub position into the cue; this is the
02:38:00 out   quickest way to pick a start frame by eye.
02:38:00 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
02:38:00 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its main window open.
02:38:00 out   2. Start VidCue. The chip should read **keyboard ready**. If it reads
02:38:00 out   **Audacity not found**, start Audacity first, then press Play again.
02:38:00 out   3. Switch **AUDACITY** off if you only want picture playback.
02:38:00 out   VidCue finds the Audacity window, gives it focus for a moment, taps Space
02:38:00 out   (the default Play/Stop shortcut in Audacity 4.0), then raises VidCue and returns
02:38:00 out   focus to it. It does not seek Audacity; set the Audacity play head to the
02:38:00 out   matching time yourself.
02:38:00 out   > This uses the X11 keyboard API and the X11 window stack. A native Wayland
02:38:00 out   > session is not supported.
02:38:00 out   ## AppImage
02:38:00 out   Build the AppImage with:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
02:38:00 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   Run it with:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   chmod +x dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:38:00 out   ./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
02:38:00 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
02:38:00 out   `./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
02:38:00 out   ## Verification flags
02:38:00 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
02:38:00 out   ```sh
02:38:00 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
02:38:00 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
02:38:00 out   # Exercise playback, the typed cue and the painted HUD, then write a JSON report.
02:38:00 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
02:38:00 out   ```
02:38:00 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
02:38:00 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
02:38:00 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
02:38:00 out   ## Project layout
02:38:00 out   | Path | Purpose |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, input, screenshot/self-test |
02:38:00 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, seek and preview |
02:38:00 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
02:38:00 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | Audacity window discovery, Space key and focus return |
02:38:00 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time parsing/formatting |
02:38:00 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
02:38:00 out   | `assets/vidcue.png` | purple app icon used by the AppImage |
02:38:00 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
02:38:00 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, cueing and sync |
02:38:00 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
02:38:00 out   ## Known limits
02:38:00 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
02:38:00 out   the typed cue is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
02:38:00 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
02:38:00 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
02:38:00 out   - Audacity 4.0's transport is driven by its Space shortcut, so Audacity must be
02:38:00 out   running on an X11 desktop and its main window must be open. Native Wayland is
02:38:00 out   not supported.
02:38:00 out   - The accent colour choice is kept for the running session; it resets to purple
02:38:00 out   when VidCue is restarted.
02:38:00 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
02:38:00 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
02:38:00 out   ## Licence
02:38:00 out   VidCue is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain under the
02:38:00 out   Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
02:38:00 out   ---
02:38:00 out   # VidCue mechanics
02:38:00 out   This sheet lists the values that shape how VidCue behaves. There is no original
02:38:00 out   program to compare against for this build, so design choices are marked
02:38:00 out   **estimated**. Values that come directly from the person's request are marked
02:38:00 out   with that source. The same values live in [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
02:38:00 out   VidCue is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means playback, cueing,
02:38:00 out   the Audacity hand-off, the compact interface and its colour settings.
02:38:00 out   ## Playback
02:38:00 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Playback rate | `1.0x` (the play head advances by the frame delta) | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Output frame rate | `30 fps` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Maximum decoded width | `1280 px` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Maximum decoded height | `720 px` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Decoded frame queue | `12 frames` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Decoder channel buffer | `8 frames` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Longest accepted frame step | `0.1 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Audio output | none (audio streams are dropped) | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Supported formats | whatever the bundled `ffmpeg` can decode | user request |
02:38:00 out   Frames are decoded by `ffmpeg` to raw RGBA, scaled with bicubic filtering to fit
02:38:00 out   inside 1280x720 while preserving aspect ratio, and sampled at a fixed 30 fps.
02:38:00 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
02:38:00 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing playback. The image is letterboxed in the
02:38:00 out   window; it is never stretched.
02:38:00 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
02:38:00 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
02:38:00 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
02:38:00 out   ## Start cue
02:38:00 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Default start cue | `0 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Cue nudge | `1 s` (`-1s` / `+1s`) | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Large cue nudge | `10 s` (`-10s` / `+10s`) | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Arrow seek step | `5 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Cue input format | `hours:minutes:seconds.milliseconds` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Default cue text | `00:00:00.000` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Start-field font | `16 px` normal, `14 px` compact, `13 px` narrow | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Start-field width | `160 px` normal, `144 px` compact, `130 px` narrow | estimated |
02:38:00 out   The start field accepts `HH:MM:SS.mmm` (for example `00:01:23.500`), and also
02:38:00 out   `HH:MM:SS`, `MM:SS`, or plain seconds. Fractional seconds are parsed from the
02:38:00 out   part after the decimal point. The value is clamped to `0..duration`.
02:38:00 out   Pressing Enter or clicking away commits it; Escape restores the previous cue.
02:38:00 out   The same cue is used in three places:
02:38:00 out   1. **Stop** rewinds the picture to the cue, so the next take starts from the same
02:38:00 out   frame.
02:38:00 out   2. **Play** after the clip has ended restarts from the cue.
02:38:00 out   3. The cue is drawn on the scrub bar as a bright marker, separate from the play
02:38:00 out   head.
02:38:00 out   The scrub bar is only a preview tool. Dragging it moves the picture and clock,
02:38:00 out   but it does not change the typed start cue. Use **Use playhead** to copy the
02:38:00 out   current scrub position into the cue. In the narrowest layout the `-10s` and
02:38:00 out   `+10s` buttons are hidden so the field and single-second nudges still fit.
02:38:00 out   ## Audacity hand-off
02:38:00 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Play key | `Space` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Stop key | `Space` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Sync enabled by default | `true` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Audacity window poll interval | `2.0 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Wait before giving focus back | `35 ms` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Focus return action | raise VidCue, then focus it | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Display server | X11 (XTest key injection) | estimated |
02:38:00 out   When **AUDACITY** is on, pressing Play or Stop finds the Audacity 4.0 window,
02:38:00 out   focuses it for a moment, taps Space (Audacity's default Play/Stop shortcut)
02:38:00 out   through XTest, waits briefly, then finds the VidCue window, raises it and gives
02:38:00 out   it focus again. There is deliberately no timeline synchronization: line up the
02:38:00 out   start cue in VidCue with the Audacity play head by hand, then start from either
02:38:00 out   side. Stop is sent even when the video had already reached its end, so the audio
02:38:00 out   transport cannot keep running after the picture stops.
02:38:00 out   The Audacity window is re-checked every 2 seconds and the chip reads **keyboard
02:38:00 out   ready** when it is found. If Audacity is not running, or the X11 keyboard
02:38:00 out   libraries are unavailable, an error is shown once per session as a toast. The
02:38:00 out   hand-off uses X11, so a native Wayland session is not supported. Audacity 4.0
02:38:00 out   was not installed on the build machine; the Space shortcut is the default
02:38:00 out   documented in the person's request.
02:38:00 out   ## Interface, layout and themes
02:38:00 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Window size | `1120x720` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Top bar height | `52 px` normal, `44 px` compact | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Transport height | `132 px` normal, `116 px` compact | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Compact breakpoint | `880 px` window width | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Narrowest layout breakpoint | `500 px` window width | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Smallest tested window | `460x360` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Toast duration | `4.5 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | File browser double-click window | `0.38 s` | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Theme choices | `orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, red` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Default theme | `purple` | user request |
02:38:00 out   | Settings panel | `226x78 px` popover under the gear | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Settings panel swatch | `24 px`, spaced `34 px` apart | estimated |
02:38:00 out   | Input | Action |
02:38:00 out   | --- | --- |
02:38:00 out   | Space | Play / Stop (Stop returns to the cue) |
02:38:00 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the cue |
02:38:00 out   | Enter (in start field) | Apply the typed start cue |
02:38:00 out   | Escape (in start field) | Discard the edit |
02:38:00 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
02:38:00 out   | Home | Jump to the cue |
02:38:00 out   | O | Open the file browser |
02:38:00 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
02:38:00 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a position, keep playing/paused state |
02:38:00 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
02:38:00 out   The layout switches to a compact transport below 880 px wide: the top bar gets
02:38:00 out   shorter, the play/stop circles shrink, the clock drops to 24 px, the Audacity
02:38:00 out   chip narrows, the start field shrinks and the hint text disappears. Below
02:38:00 out   500 px, the start field shrinks again, the large cue nudges are hidden and the
02:38:00 out   "Use playhead" button becomes "Use head". This keeps every control inside the
02:38:00 out   panel down to a small strip. The built-in file browser also drops its sidebar
02:38:00 out   below 540 px and uses a smaller footer.
02:38:00 out   The gear at the top right opens a colour popover rather than a separate settings
02:38:00 out   window. Choosing a swatch changes every accent immediately: the play button,
02:38:00 out   scrub bar, cue marker, start field outline, folder highlights and the brand
02:38:00 out   mark. The choice is kept for the running session and resets to purple on the
02:38:00 out   next launch.
02:38:00 out   ## Sources and unknowns
02:38:00 out   - The request defines the behaviour: Play/Stop control Audacity, typed start
02:38:00 out   point in hours:minutes:seconds.milliseconds, video only, broad format support,
02:38:00 out   compact controls, a settings icon with six accent colours, a purple icon and
02:38:00 out   an AppImage.
02:38:00 out   - Audacity 3.7.7 is installed on this machine and responds to an injected Space
02:38:00 out   key with its `playback/toggle-play-stop` action. Audacity 4.0 itself was not
02:38:00 out   installed, so its command compatibility is still an unknown; the person's
02:38:00 out   request says Space is its default Play/Stop shortcut. The hand-off uses
02:38:00 out   X11/XTest, so native Wayland is not covered.
02:38:00 out   - The focus-return step was tested with a stand-in X11 window named "Audacity":
02:38:00 out   after the click, the focused window was VidCue again. It raises and focuses
02:38:00 out   the window named VidCue, falling back to the window that had focus before
02:38:00 out   Audacity. It has not been tested against a real Audacity 4.0 window.
02:38:00 out   - Every numeric value is a design choice for this build, not a copied constant.
02:38:00 out   - The smallest tested window is 460x360; below that the welcome buttons can
02:38:00 out   overlap the picture edge, but the transport itself still fits.
02:38:00 out   - Decode size and frame rate trade sharpness for CPU and memory; they can be
02:38:00 out   changed in `src/player.rs` and kept in step with `mechanics.json`.
