16:02:45 out   --- MECHANICS ---
16:02:45 out   # VidCue mechanics
16:02:45 out   This sheet lists the values that shape how VidCue behaves. There is no original
16:02:45 out   program to compare against for this build, so design choices are marked
16:02:45 out   **estimated**. Values that come directly from the person's request are marked
16:02:45 out   with that source. The same values live in [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
16:02:45 out   VidCue is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means playback, cueing,
16:02:45 out   the Audacity hand-off, the compact interface and its colour settings.
16:02:45 out   ## Playback
16:02:45 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Playback rate | `1.0x` (the play head advances by the frame delta) | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Output frame rate | `30 fps` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Maximum decoded width | `1280 px` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Maximum decoded height | `720 px` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Decoded frame queue | `12 frames` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Decoder channel buffer | `8 frames` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Longest accepted frame step | `0.1 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Audio output | none (audio streams are dropped) | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Supported formats | whatever the bundled `ffmpeg` can decode | user request |
16:02:45 out   Frames are decoded by `ffmpeg` to raw RGBA, scaled with bicubic filtering to fit
16:02:45 out   inside 1280x720 while preserving aspect ratio, and sampled at a fixed 30 fps.
16:02:45 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
16:02:45 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing playback. The image is letterboxed in the
16:02:45 out   window; it is never stretched.
16:02:45 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
16:02:45 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
16:02:45 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
16:02:45 out   ## Start cue
16:02:45 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Default start cue | `0 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Arrow seek step | `5 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Cue input format | `hours:minutes:seconds.milliseconds` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Default cue text | `00:00:00.000` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Start-field font | `16 px` normal, `14 px` compact, `13 px` narrow | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Start-field width | `160 px` normal, `144 px` compact, `130 px` narrow | estimated |
16:02:45 out   The start field accepts `HH:MM:SS.mmm` (for example `00:01:23.500`), and also
16:02:45 out   `HH:MM:SS`, `MM:SS`, or plain seconds. Fractional seconds are parsed from the
16:02:45 out   part after the decimal point. The value is clamped to `0..duration`.
16:02:45 out   Pressing Enter or clicking away commits it; Escape restores the previous cue.
16:02:45 out   The same cue is used in three places:
16:02:45 out   1. **Record** rewinds the picture to the cue and plays from there while Audacity
16:02:45 out   records.
16:02:45 out   2. **Play** after the clip has ended restarts from the cue.
16:02:45 out   3. The cue is drawn on the scrub bar as a bright marker, separate from the play
16:02:45 out   head.
16:02:45 out   **Pause** holds the picture where it is, and Escape stops the picture and
16:02:45 out   returns it to the cue.
16:02:45 out   The scrub bar is only a preview tool. Dragging it moves the picture and clock,
16:02:45 out   but it does not change the typed start cue. Use **Use playhead** to copy the
16:02:45 out   current scrub position into the cue. There are no cue nudge buttons; the arrow
16:02:45 out   keys seek 5 seconds at a time.
16:02:45 out   ## Audacity hand-off
16:02:45 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Play key | `Space` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Pause key | `P` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Record key | `R` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Sync enabled by default | `true` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Audacity window poll interval | `2.0 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Wait before giving focus back | `35 ms` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Focus return action | raise VidCue, then focus it | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Display server | X11 (XTest key injection) | estimated |
16:02:45 out   When **Audacity** is on, pressing Play, Pause or Record finds the Audacity 4.0
16:02:45 out   window, focuses it for a moment, taps the matching key (Space, P or R) through
16:02:45 out   XTest, waits briefly, then finds the VidCue window, raises it and gives it focus
16:02:45 out   again. There is deliberately no timeline synchronization: line up the start cue
16:02:45 out   in VidCue with the Audacity play head by hand, then start from either side. The
16:02:45 out   key is sent even when the video has already reached its end, so the audio
16:02:45 out   transport cannot keep running after the picture stops.
16:02:45 out   The Audacity window is re-checked every 2 seconds. The dot to the left of the
16:02:45 out   **Audacity** label is green when the window is found and red when it is not.
16:02:45 out   If Audacity is not running, or the X11 keyboard libraries are unavailable, an
16:02:45 out   error is shown once per session as a toast. The hand-off uses X11, so a native
16:02:45 out   Wayland session is not supported. Audacity 4.0
16:02:45 out   was not installed on the build machine; the Space shortcut is the default
16:02:45 out   documented in the person's request.
16:02:45 out   ## Interface, layout and themes
16:02:45 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Window size | `1120x720` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Top bar height | `52 px` normal, `44 px` compact | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Transport height | `112 px` normal, `100 px` compact | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Play / Pause button | `21 px` square normal, `19 px` compact | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Record button | `21 px` red circle normal, `19 px` compact | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Master label | small caption to the right of the transport buttons | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Clock size | `13 px` normal, `12 px` compact, `10 px` narrow | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Compact breakpoint | `880 px` window width | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Narrowest layout breakpoint | `500 px` window width | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Smallest tested window | `460x360` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Toast duration | `4.5 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | File browser double-click window | `0.38 s` | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Theme choices | `orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, red` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Default theme | `purple` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Interface modes | `dark, light` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Default interface mode | `dark` | user request |
16:02:45 out   | Settings panel | `238x128 px` popover under the gear | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Settings panel swatch | `24 px`, spaced `34 px` apart | estimated |
16:02:45 out   | Input | Action |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Space | Play / Pause |
16:02:45 out   | P | Pause |
16:02:45 out   | R | Record from the cue |
16:02:45 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the cue |
16:02:45 out   | Enter (in start field) | Apply the typed start cue |
16:02:45 out   | Escape (in start field) | Discard the edit |
16:02:45 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
16:02:45 out   | Home | Jump to the cue |
16:02:45 out   | O | Open the file browser |
16:02:45 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
16:02:45 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a position, keep playing/paused state |
16:02:45 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
16:02:45 out   The layout switches to a compact transport below 880 px wide: the top bar gets
16:02:45 out   shorter, the play, pause and record buttons shrink, the clock drops to 12 px,
16:02:45 out   the Audacity chip narrows, the start field shrinks and the hint text disappears.
16:02:45 out   Below 500 px, the start field shrinks again and the "Use playhead" button
16:02:45 out   becomes "Use head". This keeps every control inside the panel down to a small
16:02:45 out   strip. The built-in file browser also drops its sidebar below 540 px and uses a
16:02:45 out   smaller footer.
16:02:45 out   The gear at the top right opens an appearance popover rather than a separate
16:02:45 out   settings window. A Dark / Light choice switches the whole interface: the dark
16:02:45 out   theme is the default, and the light theme uses the same layout with an off-white
16:02:45 out   background, dark text and a dark video stage. Choosing a swatch changes every
16:02:45 out   accent immediately: the play button, scrub bar, cue marker, start field outline,
16:02:45 out   folder highlights and the brand mark. Both choices are kept for the running
16:02:45 out   session and reset to the dark purple default on the next launch.
16:02:45 out   ## Sources and unknowns
16:02:45 out   - The request defines the behaviour: Play, Pause and Record control Audacity,
16:02:45 out   typed start point in hours:minutes:seconds.milliseconds, video only, broad
16:02:45 out   format support, compact controls, a settings icon with six accent colours and
16:02:45 out   a dark/light choice, a purple logo-matching icon and an AppImage.
16:02:45 out   - Audacity 3.7.7 is installed on this machine and responds to an injected Space
16:02:45 out   key with its `playback/toggle-play-stop` action. Audacity 4.0 itself was not
16:02:45 out   installed, so its command compatibility is still an unknown; the person's
16:02:45 out   request says Space plays, P pauses and R records. The hand-off uses X11/XTest,
16:02:45 out   so native Wayland is not covered.
16:02:45 out   - The focus-return step was tested with a stand-in X11 window named "Audacity":
16:02:45 out   after the click, the focused window was VidCue again. It raises and focuses
16:02:45 out   the window named VidCue, falling back to the window that had focus before
16:02:45 out   Audacity. It has not been tested against a real Audacity 4.0 window.
16:02:45 out   - Every numeric value is a design choice for this build, not a copied constant.
16:02:45 out   - The smallest tested window is 460x360; below that the welcome buttons can
16:02:45 out   overlap the picture edge, but the transport itself still fits.
16:02:45 out   - Decode size and frame rate trade sharpness for CPU and memory; they can be
16:02:45 out   changed in `src/player.rs` and kept in step with `mechanics.json`.
16:02:45 out   --- README ---
16:02:45 out   # VidCue
16:02:45 out   A small Linux video player for auditioning picture against an audio edit. It
16:02:45 out   shows video only (the audio track is ignored), starts from a time you type in,
16:02:45 out   and plays, pauses and records Audacity 4.0's transport by sending it Space, P
16:02:45 out   and R. After the key is sent, VidCue brings itself back to the front.
16:02:45 out   The layout is deliberately plain and compact: a large picture, a short transport
16:02:45 out   strip, one start-time field, and an Audacity link chip. There is no timeline
16:02:45 out   synchronization; you line up the video cue and the Audacity play head by hand,
16:02:45 out   then press Play. The window can be shrunk to a small strip without the controls
16:02:45 out   spilling outside the panel.
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
16:02:45 out   │ ▶ VIDCUE sample.mp4 ⚙ │
16:02:45 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
16:02:45 out   │ │
16:02:45 out   │ video picture │
16:02:45 out   │ │
16:02:45 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
16:02:45 out   │ ▶ ❚❚ ● MASTER 00:00:04 / 00:00:12 ● Audacity [ on ] │
16:02:45 out   │ ───────────────────●───────────────▲─────────────── │
16:02:45 out   │ START AT [00:00:04.000] [Use playhead] │
16:02:45 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   ## What it does
16:02:45 out   - **Plays video only.** The audio track is dropped during decode, so the picture
16:02:45 out   can run beside a separate audio session without doubling the sound.
16:02:45 out   - **Typed start cue with milliseconds.** Enter `HH:MM:SS.mmm` (for example
16:02:45 out   `00:01:23.500`), `MM:SS`, or plain seconds and press Enter. The cue is where
16:02:45 out   Record starts, where Play restarts after the end, and where Escape rewinds to.
16:02:45 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
16:02:45 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
16:02:45 out   the local `ffmpeg` supports them.
16:02:45 out   - **Audacity 4.0 transport.** Play sends Space, Pause sends P and the red Record
16:02:45 out   circle sends R to the Audacity 4.0 window. Record also starts the picture from
16:02:45 out   the typed cue. After every key VidCue raises and focuses its own window again.
16:02:45 out   The link can be switched off without stopping the video.
16:02:45 out   - **Compact controls.** The transport collapses to two short rows, with small
16:02:45 out   square play/pause buttons, a red record circle, a MASTER caption, a compact
16:02:45 out   clock and a narrower Audacity chip as the window gets smaller.
16:02:45 out   - **Dark and light interface.** The gear at the top right opens an appearance
16:02:45 out   panel with a Dark / Light choice and six accent colours: orange, yellow,
16:02:45 out   green, blue, purple and red. The dark theme is the default; the light theme
16:02:45 out   switches to an off-white background with dark text. Purple is the default
16:02:45 out   accent, matching the app icon.
16:02:45 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
16:02:45 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
16:02:45 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
16:02:45 out   ## Build and run
16:02:45 out   Requirements for building:
16:02:45 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
16:02:45 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL
16:02:45 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
16:02:45 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
16:02:45 out   AppImage script
16:02:45 out   From this folder:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   cargo build --release
16:02:45 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   Open a file directly:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   Debug build and tests:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   cargo run
16:02:45 out   cargo build
16:02:45 out   cargo test
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
16:02:45 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
16:02:45 out   ## Controls
16:02:45 out   | Input | Action |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | Space | Play / Pause |
16:02:45 out   | P | Pause |
16:02:45 out   | R | Record from the start cue |
16:02:45 out   | Escape | Stop and return to the start cue |
16:02:45 out   | Left / Right | Seek 5 seconds |
16:02:45 out   | Home | Jump to the start cue |
16:02:45 out   | O | Open the file browser |
16:02:45 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
16:02:45 out   | Enter | Apply the typed start time |
16:02:45 out   | Escape in the start field | Discard the edit |
16:02:45 out   | Drag the scrub bar | Preview a frame without changing the cue |
16:02:45 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
16:02:45 out   The **Use playhead** button copies the current scrub position into the cue; this
16:02:45 out   is the quickest way to pick a start frame by eye. There are no nudge buttons;
16:02:45 out   the arrow keys still seek 5 seconds at a time.
16:02:45 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
16:02:45 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its main window open.
16:02:45 out   2. Start VidCue. The dot beside **Audacity** turns green when the window is
16:02:45 out   found; red means it is not connected. If it is red, start Audacity first,
16:02:45 out   then press Play again.
16:02:45 out   3. Switch **Audacity** off if you only want picture playback.
16:02:45 out   VidCue finds the Audacity window, gives it focus for a moment, taps Space to
16:02:45 out   play, P to pause or R to record (Audacity 4.0's transport shortcuts), then
16:02:45 out   raises VidCue and returns focus to it. It does not seek Audacity; set the
16:02:45 out   Audacity play head to the matching time yourself.
16:02:45 out   > This uses the X11 keyboard API and the X11 window stack. A native Wayland
16:02:45 out   > session is not supported.
16:02:45 out   ## AppImage
16:02:45 out   Build the AppImage with:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
16:02:45 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   Run it with:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   chmod +x dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
16:02:45 out   ./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
16:02:45 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
16:02:45 out   `./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
16:02:45 out   ## Verification flags
16:02:45 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
16:02:45 out   ```sh
16:02:45 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
16:02:45 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
16:02:45 out   # Exercise playback, the typed cue and the painted HUD, then write a JSON report.
16:02:45 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
16:02:45 out   ```
16:02:45 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
16:02:45 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
16:02:45 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
16:02:45 out   ## Project layout
16:02:45 out   | Path | Purpose |
16:02:45 out   | --- | --- |
16:02:45 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, input, screenshot/self-test |
16:02:45 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, seek and preview |
16:02:45 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
16:02:45 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | Audacity window discovery, Space key and focus return |
16:02:45 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time parsing/formatting |
16:02:45 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
16:02:45 out   | `assets/vidcue.png` | app icon matching the in-app purple play logo |
16:02:45 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
