01:38:29 out   # VidCue
01:38:29 out   VidCue is a minimalist Linux video player that follows Audacity 4.0's playback
01:38:29 out   position. It reads the timecode Audacity already shows — `00h00m00.000s` — over
01:38:29 out   the desktop accessibility bus and scrolls the picture to the same time. It does
01:38:29 out   not send keys, take focus or care which window is on top. When Audacity plays
01:38:29 out   the picture moves; when Audacity pauses or stops the picture freezes.
01:38:29 out   The player shows video only. Audio is dropped, so it can run beside an audio
01:38:29 out   session without doubling the sound. There are no play, pause, record or start
01:38:29 out   controls: Audacity's transport is the only transport.
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
01:38:29 out   │ ▶ VIDCUE sample.mp4 ⚙ │
01:38:29 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
01:38:29 out   │ │
01:38:29 out   │ video picture │
01:38:29 out   │ │
01:38:29 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
01:38:29 out   │ ● Audacity 00:00:11.037 │
01:38:29 out   │ Following Audacity │
01:38:29 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   ## What it does
01:38:29 out   - **Follows Audacity 4.0.** The timecode is read from Audacity's accessible
01:38:29 out   `Playback position` object about thirty times a second. It is independent of
01:38:29 out   window order, focus and stacking, so the player can stay behind or in front.
01:38:29 out   - **Green / red status dot.** Green means Audacity 4.0 is running and its
01:38:29 out   timecode display is set to `00h00m00.000s`; red means either was not found.
01:38:29 out   The matching `00:00:00.000` readout is shown on the right.
01:38:29 out   - **Moves with the clock.** A changing Audacity timecode scrolls the video in
01:38:29 out   real time. A still timecode holds the current frame. A backward jump, a seek,
01:38:29 out   or Audacity's stop-and-return-to-zero moves the picture with it.
01:38:29 out   - **Video only.** Audio, subtitle and data streams are dropped during decode.
01:38:29 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
01:38:29 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
01:38:29 out   the bundled or system `ffmpeg` supports them.
01:38:29 out   - **Minimal interface.** A compact header, the picture, and one status bar.
01:38:29 out   There is no timecode entry field and no transport strip.
01:38:29 out   - **Optional appearance settings.** The gear still offers Dark / Light and six
01:38:29 out   accent colours: orange, yellow, green, blue, purple and red. Dark and purple
01:38:29 out   are the defaults.
01:38:29 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
01:38:29 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
01:38:29 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
01:38:29 out   ## Build and run
01:38:29 out   Requirements for building:
01:38:29 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
01:38:29 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL and an accessibility bus
01:38:29 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
01:38:29 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
01:38:29 out   AppImage script
01:38:29 out   From this folder:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   cargo build --release
01:38:29 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   Open a file directly:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player /path/to/take.mp4
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   Debug build and tests:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   cargo run
01:38:29 out   cargo build
01:38:29 out   cargo test
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
01:38:29 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
01:38:29 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
01:38:29 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its playback toolbar visible.
01:38:29 out   2. Set Audacity's timecode display to `00h00m00.000s` (the hour / minute /
01:38:29 out   second / millisecond form). The colon form still follows, but the status dot
01:38:29 out   stays red because it is not the requested display.
01:38:29 out   3. Start VidCue and open a video. The dot turns green when Audacity's timecode
01:38:29 out   object is found.
01:38:29 out   4. Play, pause or stop in Audacity. VidCue follows the readout; it never seeks
01:38:29 out   Audacity and never brings a window to the front.
01:38:29 out   If the dot stays red, make sure the desktop accessibility bus is running
01:38:29 out   (`org.a11y.Bus`) and that Audacity's playback toolbar is shown. Minimal window
01:38:29 out   managers sometimes start without it.
01:38:29 out   ## Controls
01:38:29 out   | Input | Action |
01:38:29 out   | --- | --- |
01:38:29 out   | O | Open the file browser |
01:38:29 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
01:38:29 out   | Escape | Close the settings panel |
01:38:29 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
01:38:29 out   | Gear button | Appearance panel |
01:38:29 out   There are deliberately no playback buttons and no start-time field.
01:38:29 out   ## AppImage
01:38:29 out   Build the AppImage with:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
01:38:29 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   Run it with:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   chmod +x dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
01:38:29 out   ./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
01:38:29 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
01:38:29 out   `./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
01:38:29 out   ## Verification flags
01:38:29 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
01:38:29 out   ```sh
01:38:29 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
01:38:29 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --screenshot shots/now.png
01:38:29 out   # Drive the picture from a simulated Audacity clock, then write a JSON report.
01:38:29 out   ./target/release/cue-video-player --self-test shots/test.json
01:38:29 out   ```
01:38:29 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
01:38:29 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
01:38:29 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
01:38:29 out   ## Project layout
01:38:29 out   | Path | Purpose |
01:38:29 out   | --- | --- |
01:38:29 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, screenshot/self-test |
01:38:29 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, follow and hold |
01:38:29 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
01:38:29 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | AT-SPI timecode discovery, polling and movement detection |
01:38:29 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time formatting |
01:38:29 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
01:38:29 out   | `assets/vidcue.png` | app icon matching the in-app purple play logo |
01:38:29 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
01:38:29 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, follow and layout |
01:38:29 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
01:38:29 out   ## Known limits
01:38:29 out   - The desktop accessibility bus must be available. VidCue can switch it on, but
01:38:29 out   it cannot create one where none is installed.
01:38:29 out   - Audacity 4.0.2 on Linux is the version observed while building. The accessible
01:38:29 out   object name used here is `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`. A translated
01:38:29 out   label still works as long as the name ends in a readable clock, and a bare
01:38:29 out   clock name also works; a future Audacity release could change the format.
01:38:29 out   - Audacity's stop action returns its play head to the start of the selection or
01:38:29 out   project; VidCue follows that position, so the picture returns with it.
01:38:29 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
01:38:29 out   the picture is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
01:38:29 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
01:38:29 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
01:38:29 out   - The dark/light choice and accent colour are kept for the running session; they
01:38:29 out   reset to dark and purple when VidCue is restarted.
01:38:29 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
01:38:29 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
01:38:29 out   ## Licence
01:38:29 out   VidCue is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain under the
01:38:29 out   Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
