01:39:17 out   //! Context creation configuration
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! A [`Conf`] struct is used to describe a hardware and platform specific setup,
01:39:17 out   //! mostly video display settings.
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! ## High DPI rendering
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! You can set the [`Conf::high_dpi`] flag during initialization to request
01:39:17 out   //! a full-resolution framebuffer on HighDPI displays. The default behaviour
01:39:17 out   //! is `high_dpi = false`, this means that the application will
01:39:17 out   //! render to a lower-resolution framebuffer on HighDPI displays and the
01:39:17 out   //! rendered content will be upscaled by the window system composer.
01:39:17 out   //! In a HighDPI scenario, you still request the same window size during
01:39:17 out   //! [`miniquad::start`][super::start], but the framebuffer sizes returned by
01:39:17 out   //! [`screen_size`] will be scaled up according to the DPI scaling ratio.
01:39:17 out   //! You can also get a DPI scaling factor with the function [`dpi_scale`].
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! Here's an example on a Mac with Retina display:
01:39:17 out   //! ```ignore
01:39:17 out   //! Conf {
01:39:17 out   //! width = 640,
01:39:17 out   //! height = 480,
01:39:17 out   //! high_dpi = true,
01:39:17 out   //! .. Default::default()
01:39:17 out   //! };
01:39:17 out   //! ```
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! The functions [`screen_size`] and [`dpi_scale`] will return the following values:
01:39:17 out   //! ```bash
01:39:17 out   //! screen_size -> (1280, 960)
01:39:17 out   //! dpi_scale -> 2.0
01:39:17 out   //! ```
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! If the `high_dpi` flag is false, or you're not running on a Retina display,
01:39:17 out   //! the values would be:
01:39:17 out   //! ```bash
01:39:17 out   //! screen_size -> (640, 480)
01:39:17 out   //! dpi_scale -> 1.0
01:39:17 out   //! ```
01:39:17 out   //!
01:39:17 out   //! [`dpi_scale`]: super::window::dpi_scale
01:39:17 out   //! [`screen_size`]: super::window::screen_size
01:39:17 out   /// Specifies how to load an OpenGL context on X11 in Linux.
01:39:17 out   #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
01:39:17 out   pub enum LinuxX11Gl {
01:39:17 out   /// Use `libGLX.so` (or `libGLX.so.0`) exclusively. Panics if unavailable.
01:39:17 out   GLXOnly,
01:39:17 out   /// Use `libEGL.so` (or `libEGL.so.0`) exclusively. Panics if unavailable.
01:39:17 out   EGLOnly,
01:39:17 out   /// Prefer `libGLX`; fall back to `libEGL` if `libGLX` is unavailable.
01:39:17 out   /// This is the default choice.
01:39:17 out   #[default]
01:39:17 out   GLXWithEGLFallback,
01:39:17 out   /// Prefer `libEGL`; fall back to `libGLX` if `libEGL` is unavailable.
01:39:17 out   EGLWithGLXFallback,
01:39:17 out   }
01:39:17 out   /// On Linux, the backend used for windowing and event handling.
01:39:17 out   ///
01:39:17 out   /// Defaults to `X11Only`. The Wayland implementation is currently unstable
01:39:17 out   #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
01:39:17 out   pub enum LinuxBackend {
01:39:17 out   /// Use only the X11 backend. Panics if unavailable. This is the default choice.
01:39:17 out   #[default]
01:39:17 out   X11Only,
