01:39:53 out   ---
01:39:53 out   # VidCue
01:39:53 out   VidCue is a minimalist Linux video player that follows Audacity 4.0's playback
01:39:53 out   position. It reads the timecode Audacity already shows — `00h00m00.000s` — over
01:39:53 out   the desktop accessibility bus and scrolls the picture to the same time. It does
01:39:53 out   not send keys, take focus or care which window is on top. When Audacity plays
01:39:53 out   the picture moves; when Audacity pauses or stops the picture freezes.
01:39:53 out   The player shows video only. Audio is dropped, so it can run beside an audio
01:39:53 out   session without doubling the sound. There are no play, pause, record or start
01:39:53 out   controls: Audacity's transport is the only transport.
01:39:53 out   ```
01:39:53 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
01:39:53 out   │ ▶ VIDCUE sample.mp4 ⚙ │
01:39:53 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
01:39:53 out   │ │
01:39:53 out   │ video picture │
01:39:53 out   │ │
01:39:53 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
01:39:53 out   │ ● Audacity 00:00:11.037 │
01:39:53 out   │ Following Audacity │
01:39:53 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
01:39:53 out   ```
01:39:53 out   ## What it does
01:39:53 out   - **Follows Audacity 4.0.** The timecode is read from Audacity's accessible
01:39:53 out   `Playback position` object about thirty times a second. It is independent of
01:39:53 out   window order, focus and stacking, so the player can stay behind or in front.
01:39:53 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
01:39:53 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
01:39:53 out   ## Project layout
01:39:53 out   | Path | Purpose |
01:39:53 out   | --- | --- |
01:39:53 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, screenshot/self-test |
01:39:53 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, follow and hold |
01:39:53 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
01:39:53 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | AT-SPI timecode discovery, polling and movement detection |
01:39:53 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time formatting |
01:39:53 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
01:39:53 out   | `assets/vidcue.png` | app icon matching the in-app purple play logo |
01:39:53 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
01:39:53 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, follow and layout |
01:39:53 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
01:39:53 out   ## Known limits
01:39:53 out   - The desktop accessibility bus must be available. VidCue can switch it on, but
01:39:53 out   it cannot create one where none is installed.
01:39:53 out   - Audacity 4.0.2 on Linux is the version observed while building. The accessible
01:39:53 out   object name used here is `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`. A translated
01:39:53 out   label still works as long as the name ends in a readable clock, and a bare
01:39:53 out   clock name also works; a future Audacity release could change the format.
01:39:53 out   - Audacity's stop action returns its play head to the start of the selection or
01:39:53 out   project; VidCue follows that position, so the picture returns with it.
01:39:53 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
01:39:53 out   the picture is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
01:39:53 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
01:39:53 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
01:39:53 out   - The dark/light choice and accent colour are kept for the running session; they
01:39:53 out   reset to dark and purple when VidCue is restarted.
01:39:53 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
01:39:53 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
01:39:53 out   ## Licence
01:39:53 out   VidCue is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain under the
01:39:53 out   Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
01:39:53 out   ---
01:39:53 out   # VidCue mechanics
01:39:53 out   This sheet lists the values that shape VidCue. There is no original program to
01:39:53 out   compare against, so design choices are marked **estimated**. Values taken from
01:39:53 out   the person's requests are marked with that source; values read from Audacity on
01:39:53 out   the build machine are marked **observed**. The same values live in
01:39:53 out   [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
01:39:53 out   VidCue is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means following
01:39:53 out   Audacity 4.0's timecode, playback, the status bar and the small appearance
01:39:53 out   panel.
01:39:53 out   ## Following Audacity
01:39:53 out   The person asked for a player with no transport of its own: Audacity 4.0's
01:39:53 out   timecode is the clock, the player is the slave, and the hierarchy of desktop
01:39:53 out   windows must not matter.
01:39:53 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
01:39:53 out   | --- | --- | --- |
01:39:53 out   | Timecode source | AT-SPI accessible name `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`; any short name ending in a clock also works | observed (Audacity 4.0.2) / fallback |
01:39:53 out   | Timecode readout format | `HH:MM:SS.mmm`, e.g. `00:00:11.037` | user request |
01:39:53 out   | Required Audacity display | `00h00m00.000s` for a green dot | user request |
01:39:53 out   | Green dot | Audacity found and timecode uses the hour/minute/second display | user request |
01:39:53 out   | Red dot | Audacity not found, or its timecode uses another display | user request |
01:39:53 out   | Window order | irrelevant; no focus or key injection | user request |
01:39:53 out   | Timecode poll interval | `33 ms` | estimated |
01:39:53 out   | Movement window | `220 ms` | estimated |
01:39:53 out   | Movement threshold | `0.0005 s` | estimated |
01:39:53 out   | Timecode jump threshold | `0.4 s` (restart the decoder nearby) | estimated |
01:39:53 out   and reset to dark and purple on the next launch.
01:39:53 out   ## Sources and unknowns
01:39:53 out   - The latest request defines this version: no transport buttons, no start-time
01:39:53 out   field, no Audacity switch, a live `00:00:00.000` readout, a green/red dot for
01:39:53 out   Audacity and its `00h00m00.000s` display, a minimalist slave player, and
01:39:53 out   window-order independence.
01:39:53 out   - Audacity 4.0.2 was running on the build machine. Its accessible tree exposes
01:39:53 out   the name `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`, and the value advanced while
01:39:53 out   Audacity played. VidCue's live trace followed it and reset with Audacity's
01:39:53 out   stop-to-zero.
01:39:53 out   - Audacity 4.0 on other Linux desktops, and any future Audacity version, could
01:39:53 out   expose a different accessible name. VidCue falls back to any short accessible
01:39:53 out   name that ends in a clock, so translated labels work, but a future release
01:39:53 out   could change the clock format itself.
01:39:53 out   - The accessibility bus must be running. VidCue switches `IsEnabled` on, but
01:39:53 out   cannot install a missing AT-SPI stack.
01:39:53 out   - Every numeric layout value is a design choice for this build, not a copied
01:39:53 out   constant.
01:39:53 out   - Decode size and frame rate trade sharpness for CPU and memory; they can be
01:39:53 out   changed in `src/player.rs` and kept in step with `mechanics.json`.
