17:46:25 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
17:46:25 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing the follow. The image is letterboxed in
17:46:25 out   the window; it is never stretched.
17:46:25 out   +`ffmpeg` decodes as fast as the machine allows, so the 12-frame queue keeps the
17:46:25 out   +*earliest* frames and stops reading from the decoder while it is full. Draining
17:46:25 out   +every available frame would throw away the pictures the clock is at and leave
17:46:25 out   +only frames from seconds in the future. When Audacity runs past the end of a
17:46:25 out   +short clip, the player parks on the last frame instead of restarting the
17:46:25 out   +decoder on every timecode tick.
17:46:25 out   +
17:46:25 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
17:46:25 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
17:46:25 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
