17:57:58 out   --- README ---
17:57:58 out   # VIDSYNC
17:57:58 out   VIDSYNC is a minimalist Linux video player that follows Audacity 4.0's playback
17:57:58 out   position. It reads the timecode Audacity already shows — `00h00m00.000s` — over
17:57:58 out   the desktop accessibility bus and scrolls the picture to the same time. It does
17:57:58 out   not send keys, take focus or care which window is on top. When Audacity plays
17:57:58 out   the picture moves; when Audacity pauses or stops the picture freezes.
17:57:58 out   The player shows video only. Audio is dropped, so it can run beside an audio
17:57:58 out   session without doubling the sound. There are no play, pause, record or start
17:57:58 out   controls: Audacity's transport is the only transport.
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
17:57:58 out   │ ▶ VIDSYNC sample.mp4 ⚙ │
17:57:58 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
17:57:58 out   │ │
17:57:58 out   │ video picture │
17:57:58 out   │ │
17:57:58 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
17:57:58 out   │ ● Audacity 00:00:11.037 │
17:57:58 out   │ Following Audacity │
17:57:58 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   ## What it does
17:57:58 out   - **Follows Audacity 4.0.** The timecode is read from Audacity's accessible
17:57:58 out   `Playback position` object about thirty times a second. It is independent of
17:57:58 out   window order, focus and stacking, so the player can stay behind or in front.
17:57:58 out   - **Green / red status dot.** Green means Audacity 4.0 is running and its
17:57:58 out   timecode display is set to `00h00m00.000s`; red means either was not found.
17:57:58 out   The matching `00:00:00.000` readout is shown on the right.
17:57:58 out   - **Moves with the clock.** A changing Audacity timecode scrolls the video in
17:57:58 out   real time. A still timecode holds the current frame. A backward jump, a seek,
17:57:58 out   or Audacity's stop-and-return-to-zero moves the picture with it.
17:57:58 out   - **Video only.** Audio, subtitle and data streams are dropped during decode.
17:57:58 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
17:57:58 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
17:57:58 out   the bundled or system `ffmpeg` supports them.
17:57:58 out   - **Minimal interface.** A compact header, the picture, and one status bar.
17:57:58 out   There is no timecode entry field and no transport strip.
17:57:58 out   - **Optional appearance settings.** The gear offers Dark / Light and six accent
17:57:58 out   colours: orange, yellow, green, blue, purple and red. Dark and purple are the
17:57:58 out   defaults, and the chosen mode and colour are remembered on the next launch.
17:57:58 out   The menu, desktop and window icons use the same purple play tile shown beside
17:57:58 out   the name.
17:57:58 out   - **File browser and drag-and-drop.** No GTK dialogs are needed.
17:57:58 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
17:57:58 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
17:57:58 out   ## Build and run
17:57:58 out   Requirements for building:
17:57:58 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
17:57:58 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL and an accessibility bus
17:57:58 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
17:57:58 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
17:57:58 out   AppImage script
17:57:58 out   From this folder:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   cargo build --release
17:57:58 out   ./target/release/vidsync
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   Open a file directly:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   ./target/release/vidsync /path/to/take.mp4
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   Debug build and tests:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   cargo run
17:57:58 out   cargo build
17:57:58 out   cargo test
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
17:57:58 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
17:57:58 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
17:57:58 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its playback toolbar visible.
17:57:58 out   2. Set Audacity's timecode display to `00h00m00.000s` (the hour / minute /
17:57:58 out   second / millisecond form). The colon form still follows, but the status dot
17:57:58 out   stays red because it is not the requested display.
17:57:58 out   3. Start VIDSYNC and open a video. The dot turns green when Audacity's timecode
17:57:58 out   object is found.
17:57:58 out   4. Play, pause or stop in Audacity. VIDSYNC follows the readout; it never seeks
17:57:58 out   Audacity and never brings a window to the front.
17:57:58 out   If the dot stays red, make sure the desktop accessibility bus is running
17:57:58 out   (`org.a11y.Bus`) and that Audacity's playback toolbar is shown. Minimal window
17:57:58 out   managers sometimes start without it.
17:57:58 out   ## Controls
17:57:58 out   | Input | Action |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | O | Open the file browser |
17:57:58 out   | F | Toggle fullscreen |
17:57:58 out   | Escape | Close the settings panel |
17:57:58 out   | Drop a file on the window | Open it |
17:57:58 out   | Gear button | Appearance panel |
17:57:58 out   There are deliberately no playback buttons and no start-time field.
17:57:58 out   ## AppImage
17:57:58 out   Build the AppImage with:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
17:57:58 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   Run it with:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   chmod +x dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
17:57:58 out   ./dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
17:57:58 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
17:57:58 out   `./dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
17:57:58 out   ## Verification flags
17:57:58 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
17:57:58 out   ```sh
17:57:58 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
17:57:58 out   ./target/release/vidsync --screenshot shots/now.png
17:57:58 out   # Drive the picture from a simulated Audacity clock, then write a JSON report.
17:57:58 out   ./target/release/vidsync --self-test shots/test.json
17:57:58 out   ```
17:57:58 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
17:57:58 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
17:57:58 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
17:57:58 out   ## Project layout
17:57:58 out   | Path | Purpose |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, screenshot/self-test |
17:57:58 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, follow and hold |
17:57:58 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
17:57:58 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | AT-SPI timecode discovery, polling and movement detection |
17:57:58 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time formatting |
17:57:58 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated demo clip used by the welcome screen |
17:57:58 out   | `assets/vidsync.png` | app icon matching the in-app purple play logo |
17:57:58 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
17:57:58 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, follow and layout |
17:57:58 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
17:57:58 out   ## Known limits
17:57:58 out   - The desktop accessibility bus must be available. VIDSYNC can switch it on, but
17:57:58 out   it cannot create one where none is installed.
17:57:58 out   - Audacity 4.0.2 on Linux is the version observed while building. The accessible
17:57:58 out   object name used here is `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`. A translated
17:57:58 out   label still works as long as the name ends in a readable clock, and a bare
17:57:58 out   clock name also works; a future Audacity release could change the format.
17:57:58 out   - Audacity's stop action returns its play head to the start of the selection or
17:57:58 out   project; VIDSYNC follows that position, so the picture returns with it.
17:57:58 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
17:57:58 out   the picture is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
17:57:58 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
17:57:58 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
17:57:58 out   - The dark/light choice and accent colour are stored in
17:57:58 out   `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vidsync/settings.json` (usually under `.config`) and restored
17:57:58 out   on the next launch.
17:57:58 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
17:57:58 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
17:57:58 out   ## Licence
17:57:58 out   VIDSYNC is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain under the
17:57:58 out   Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
17:57:58 out   --- MECHANICS ---
17:57:58 out   # VIDSYNC mechanics
17:57:58 out   This sheet lists the values that shape VIDSYNC. There is no original program to
17:57:58 out   compare against, so design choices are marked **estimated**. Values taken from
17:57:58 out   the person's requests are marked with that source; values read from Audacity on
17:57:58 out   the build machine are marked **observed**. The same values live in
17:57:58 out   [`mechanics.json`](mechanics.json).
17:57:58 out   VIDSYNC is a video player, not a game. "Mechanics" here means following
17:57:58 out   Audacity 4.0's timecode, playback, the status bar and the small appearance
17:57:58 out   panel.
17:57:58 out   ## Following Audacity
17:57:58 out   The person asked for a player with no transport of its own: Audacity 4.0's
17:57:58 out   timecode is the clock, the player is the slave, and the hierarchy of desktop
17:57:58 out   windows must not matter.
17:57:58 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode source | AT-SPI accessible name `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`; any short name ending in a clock also works | observed (Audacity 4.0.2) / fallback |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode readout format | `HH:MM:SS.mmm`, e.g. `00:00:11.037` | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Required Audacity display | `00h00m00.000s` for a green dot | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Green dot | Audacity found and timecode uses the hour/minute/second display | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Red dot | Audacity not found, or its timecode uses another display | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Window order | irrelevant; no focus or key injection | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode poll interval | `33 ms` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Movement window | `220 ms` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Movement threshold | `0.0005 s` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode jump threshold | `0.4 s` (restart the decoder nearby) | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Still-frame refresh threshold | `0.02 s` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Audacity rediscovery interval | `12 s` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Failed discovery retry | `1 s` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Audacity version observed | `4.0.2` | observed (build machine) |
17:57:58 out   | Display server | any Linux desktop with AT-SPI, X11 or Wayland | observed / estimated |
17:57:58 out   The background thread walks the accessibility tree once, caches the node whose
17:57:58 out   name starts with `Playback position`, and reads it about thirty times a second.
17:57:58 out   A value that changes within the movement window marks Audacity as playing. The
17:57:58 out   UI then either follows the clock or holds a still frame. Nothing is sent to
17:57:58 out   Audacity and no window is raised, so the two applications can be stacked in any
17:57:58 out   order.
17:57:58 out   When Audacity is playing, VIDSYNC follows each reading. A drift of more than
17:57:58 out   `0.4 s` restarts `ffmpeg` from the new position; smaller drift is absorbed by
17:57:58 out   the decoder already running. When the clock stops changing, the decoder is
17:57:58 out   stopped and a still frame is fetched for the held position. If Audacity jumps
17:57:58 out   backwards, or its stop action returns the play head to zero, the picture follows
17:57:58 out   that jump.
17:57:58 out   ## Playback
17:57:58 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | Playback rate | `1.0x`, paced by the external timecode | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Output frame rate | `30 fps` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Maximum decoded width | `1280 px` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Maximum decoded height | `720 px` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Decoded frame queue | `12 frames` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Decoder channel buffer | `8 frames` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Longest accepted frame step | `0.1 s` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Audio output | none (audio streams are dropped) | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Supported formats | whatever the bundled `ffmpeg` can decode | user request |
17:57:58 out   Frames are decoded by `ffmpeg` to raw RGBA, scaled with bicubic filtering to fit
17:57:58 out   inside 1280x720 while preserving aspect ratio, and sampled at a fixed 30 fps.
17:57:58 out   The player displays the newest frame whose timestamp has been reached, so a slow
17:57:58 out   decode skips frames instead of slowing the follow. The image is letterboxed in
17:57:58 out   the window; it is never stretched.
17:57:58 out   `ffmpeg` decodes as fast as the machine allows, so the 12-frame queue keeps the
17:57:58 out   *earliest* frames and stops reading from the decoder while it is full. Draining
17:57:58 out   every available frame would throw away the pictures the clock is at and leave
17:57:58 out   only frames from seconds in the future. When Audacity runs past the end of a
17:57:58 out   short clip, the player parks on the last frame instead of restarting the
17:57:58 out   decoder on every timecode tick.
17:57:58 out   Audio, subtitles and data streams are explicitly dropped (`-an -sn -dn`). Video
17:57:58 out   codecs and containers are whatever the bundled/system `ffmpeg` can decode:
17:57:58 out   MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM, AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and more.
17:57:58 out   ## Controls and transport
17:57:58 out   The person asked to remove the start-time field, the Audacity on/off switch and
17:57:58 out   the play, pause and record buttons. Audacity is the only transport.
17:57:58 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | Play button | none | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Pause button | none | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Record button | none | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Start-time field | none | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Audacity toggle switch | none | user request |
17:57:58 out   | Open shortcut | `O` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Fullscreen shortcut | `F` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Close settings | `Escape` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Drag and drop | supported | estimated |
17:57:58 out   The header keeps only the wordmark, the current file name, an Open button and
17:57:58 out   the gear. The body is the picture and the status bar. The status bar keeps the
17:57:58 out   `Audacity` label, its green/red dot and the `00:00:00.000` readout.
17:57:58 out   ## Interface, layout and themes
17:57:58 out   | Value | Setting | Source |
17:57:58 out   | --- | --- | --- |
17:57:58 out   | Window size | `1120x720` | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Top bar height | `52 px` normal, `44 px` compact | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Status bar height | `70 px` normal, `58 px` compact | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Status bar side padding | `18 px` normal, `12 px` compact | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode font | `32 px` normal, `22 px` compact, `18 px` narrow | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Timecode area width | `280 px` normal, `200 px` compact, `160 px` narrow | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Audacity label | `14 px` normal, `12 px` compact | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Status dot | `4.5 px` radius, with a glow while following | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Compact breakpoint | `880 px` window width | estimated |
17:57:58 out   | Narrowest layout breakpoint | `500 px` window width | estimated |
