02:04:26 out   **What it does**
02:04:26 out   - Plays video only — audio is discarded.
02:04:26 out   - Follows Audacity 4.0’s live `00h00m00.000s` readout through AT-SPI, so window order doesn’t matter.
02:04:26 out   - Advances the picture while Audacity plays and holds the current frame when it pauses.
02:04:26 out   - Follows Audacity’s stop-to-zero back to the start.
02:04:26 out   - Shows the matching `00:00:00.000` readout, plus a green dot when Audacity is found and using the expected display; red otherwise.
02:04:26 out   - Keeps the Open button, drag-and-drop, file browser, fullscreen, dark/light themes, and six accent colours.
02:04:26 out   - Falls back to any short accessible name ending in a clock, so translated Audacity labels still work.
02:04:26 out   **Verified**
02:04:26 out   - 8 unit tests pass.
02:04:26 out   - Release binary self-test: move, act, and HUD all pass.
02:04:26 out   - AppImage self-test: move, act, and HUD all pass.
02:04:26 out   - Live check against Audacity 4.0.2: green dot confirmed, video and clock follow the readout.
02:04:26 out   **Files**
02:04:26 out   - AppImage: `/home/carl/Documents/Craft Anything/i-want-to-create-a/app/dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage` (56 MB)
02:04:26 out   - Release binary: `/home/carl/Documents/Craft Anything/i-want-to-create-a/app/target/release/cue-video-player`
02:04:26 out   - Screenshots and test results: `shots/final*.png`, `shots/final-test.json`, `shots/final-appimage-test.json`
02:04:26 out   - Updated docs: `README.md`, `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json`
02:04:26 out   Run it with:
02:04:26 out   ```bash
02:04:26 out   ./dist/VidCue-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:04:26 out   ```
02:04:26 out   The main remaining limitation is that Audacity must expose its playback position through the desktop accessibility bus. VidCue enables it when possible but cannot install a missing AT-SPI stack.
