02:28:49 out   [0m$ [0msed -n '1,210p' README.md
02:28:49 out   # VIDSYNC
02:28:49 out   VIDSYNC is a minimalist Linux video player that follows Audacity 4.0's playback
02:28:49 out   position. It reads the timecode Audacity already shows — `00h00m00.000s` — over
02:28:49 out   the desktop accessibility bus and scrolls the picture to the same time. It does
02:28:49 out   not send keys, take focus or care which window is on top. When Audacity plays
02:28:49 out   the picture moves; when Audacity pauses or stops the picture freezes.
02:28:49 out   The player shows video only. Audio is dropped, so it can run beside an audio
02:28:49 out   session without doubling the sound. There are no play, pause, record or start
02:28:49 out   controls: Audacity's transport is the only transport.
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
02:28:49 out   │ ▶ VIDSYNC sample.mp4 ⚙ │
02:28:49 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
02:28:49 out   │ │
02:28:49 out   │ video picture │
02:28:49 out   │ │
02:28:49 out   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
02:28:49 out   │ ● Audacity 00:00:11.037 │
02:28:49 out   │ Following Audacity │
02:28:49 out   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   ## What it does
02:28:49 out   - **Follows Audacity 4.0.** The timecode is read from Audacity's accessible
02:28:49 out   `Playback position` object about thirty times a second. It is independent of
02:28:49 out   window order, focus and stacking, so the player can stay behind or in front.
02:28:49 out   - **Green / red status dot.** Green means Audacity 4.0 is running and its
02:28:49 out   timecode display is set to `00h00m00.000s`; red means either was not found.
02:28:49 out   The matching `00:00:00.000` readout is shown on the right.
02:28:49 out   - **Moves with the clock.** A changing Audacity timecode scrolls the video in
02:28:49 out   real time. A still timecode holds the current frame. A backward jump, a seek,
02:28:49 out   or Audacity's stop-and-return-to-zero moves the picture with it.
02:28:49 out   - **Video only.** Audio, subtitle and data streams are dropped during decode.
02:28:49 out   - **Most formats.** Decoding is delegated to `ffmpeg`, so MP4, MKV, MOV, WebM,
02:28:49 out   AVI, MPEG, TS, MTS, OGV, FLV, WMV and other common containers work as long as
02:28:49 out   the bundled or system `ffmpeg` supports them.
02:28:49 out   - **Minimal interface.** A compact header, the picture, and one status bar.
02:28:49 out   The picture runs edge-to-edge between the header and the status bar, with
02:28:49 out   black letterbox or pillarbox bars protecting its aspect ratio. There is no
02:28:49 out   timecode entry field and no transport strip.
02:28:49 out   - **Optional appearance and window settings.** The gear offers Dark / Light,
02:28:49 out   six accent colours (orange, yellow, green, blue, purple and red), and a
02:28:49 out   **Keep on top** switch that holds the player above other windows. Dark,
02:28:49 out   purple and off are the defaults, and all three choices are remembered on the
02:28:49 out   next launch. The menu, desktop and window icons use the same purple play tile
02:28:49 out   shown beside the name.
02:28:49 out   - **File browser only.** Videos are opened from the built-in browser. There is
02:28:49 out   no drag-and-drop target, no sample-clip shortcut and no empty-stage prompt;
02:28:49 out   when nothing is loaded the picture area stays black. No GTK dialogs are
02:28:49 out   needed.
02:28:49 out   - **Ships as an AppImage** with `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` inside, so the target machine
02:28:49 out   does not need ffmpeg installed.
02:28:49 out   ## Build and run
02:28:49 out   Requirements for building:
02:28:49 out   - Rust and Cargo (edition 2021)
02:28:49 out   - A Linux desktop with OpenGL and an accessibility bus
02:28:49 out   - `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` for running from a normal build
02:28:49 out   - `curl`, `tar`, `xz` and `appimagetool` (downloaded automatically) for the
02:28:49 out   AppImage script
02:28:49 out   From this folder:
02:28:49 out   ```sh
02:28:49 out   cargo build --release
02:28:49 out   ./target/release/vidsync
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   Videos are opened from the Open video button inside the player.
02:28:49 out   Debug build and tests:
02:28:49 out   ```sh
02:28:49 out   cargo run
02:28:49 out   cargo build
02:28:49 out   cargo test
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   The app looks for `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` next to its executable first, then on
02:28:49 out   `PATH`. Override them with `CUE_FFMPEG` and `CUE_FFPROBE` if needed.
02:28:49 out   ## Connecting Audacity 4.0
02:28:49 out   1. Start Audacity 4.0 and leave its playback toolbar visible.
02:28:49 out   2. Set Audacity's timecode display to `00h00m00.000s` (the hour / minute /
02:28:49 out   second / millisecond form). The colon form still follows, but the status dot
02:28:49 out   stays red because it is not the requested display.
02:28:49 out   3. Start VIDSYNC and open a video. The dot turns green when Audacity's timecode
02:28:49 out   object is found.
02:28:49 out   4. Play, pause or stop in Audacity. VIDSYNC follows the readout; it never seeks
02:28:49 out   Audacity and never brings a window to the front.
02:28:49 out   If the dot stays red, make sure the desktop accessibility bus is running
02:28:49 out   (`org.a11y.Bus`) and that Audacity's playback toolbar is shown. Minimal window
02:28:49 out   managers sometimes start without it.
02:28:49 out   ## Controls
02:28:49 out   | Input | Action |
02:28:49 out   | --- | --- |
02:28:49 out   | O or the header Open video button | Open the file browser |
02:28:49 out   | F or the header fullscreen button | Toggle fullscreen |
02:28:49 out   | Escape | Exit fullscreen, or close the settings panel |
02:28:49 out   | Gear button | Settings panel: Dark / Light, Keep on top, accent colour |
02:28:49 out   Fullscreen shows only the video on a black background; the header, status bar
02:28:49 out   and all other overlays are hidden. The picture keeps its aspect ratio, so black
02:28:49 out   bars appear when the screen shape differs from the video.
02:28:49 out   There are deliberately no playback buttons, no start-time field, no drag and
02:28:49 out   drop, and no sample-clip shortcut. The empty stage has no text or buttons; use
02:28:49 out   the Open video button in the header to browse for a file.
02:28:49 out   ## AppImage
02:28:49 out   Build the AppImage with:
02:28:49 out   ```sh
02:28:49 out   ./scripts/build-appimage.sh
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   The script downloads the static `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` build and `appimagetool`,
02:28:49 out   stages an AppDir under `build/`, and writes:
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   Run it with:
02:28:49 out   ```sh
02:28:49 out   chmod +x dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:28:49 out   ./dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   The AppImage contains its own ffmpeg and does not require FUSE to build. If your
02:28:49 out   system does not have FUSE, run it with
02:28:49 out   `./dist/VIDSYNC-1.0.0-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
02:28:49 out   ## Verification flags
02:28:49 out   The binary supports two non-interactive modes used by the build checks:
02:28:49 out   ```sh
02:28:49 out   # Draw the app for ~120 frames, save a PNG, and exit.
02:28:49 out   ./target/release/vidsync --screenshot shots/now.png
02:28:49 out   # Drive the picture from a simulated Audacity clock, then write a JSON report.
02:28:49 out   ./target/release/vidsync --self-test shots/test.json
02:28:49 out   ```
02:28:49 out   The self-test writes `{"version": 1, "steps": [...]}` with `move`, `act` and
02:28:49 out   `hud` steps. Both modes use the embedded sample clip extracted to the system
02:28:49 out   temporary directory, so they work without any external media.
02:28:49 out   ## Project layout
02:28:49 out   | Path | Purpose |
02:28:49 out   | --- | --- |
02:28:49 out   | `src/main.rs` | app state, compact layout, drawing, screenshot/self-test |
02:28:49 out   | `src/player.rs` | ffprobe metadata, ffmpeg decode loop, follow and hold |
02:28:49 out   | `src/browser.rs` | in-app file browser |
02:28:49 out   | `src/audacity.rs` | AT-SPI timecode discovery, polling and movement detection |
02:28:49 out   | `src/ui.rs` | palette, themes, fonts, shapes, time formatting |
02:28:49 out   | `assets/sample.mp4` | small generated clip used only by the screenshot and self-test checks |
02:28:49 out   | `assets/vidsync.png` | app icon matching the in-app purple play logo |
02:28:49 out   | `assets/fonts/` | bundled DejaVu fonts (Bitstream Vera licence included) |
02:28:49 out   | `MECHANICS.md`, `mechanics.json` | values behind playback, follow and layout |
02:28:49 out   | `scripts/build-appimage.sh` | reproducible AppImage build |
02:28:49 out   ## Known limits
02:28:49 out   - The desktop accessibility bus must be available. VIDSYNC can switch it on, but
02:28:49 out   it cannot create one where none is installed.
02:28:49 out   - Audacity 4.0.2 on Linux is the version observed while building. The accessible
02:28:49 out   object name used here is `Playback position:00 h 00 m 00.000 s`. A translated
02:28:49 out   label still works as long as the name ends in a readable clock, and a bare
02:28:49 out   clock name also works; a future Audacity release could change the format.
02:28:49 out   - Audacity's stop action returns its play head to the start of the selection or
02:28:49 out   project; VIDSYNC follows that position, so the picture returns with it.
02:28:49 out   - Frame-accurate seeking depends on keyframes and on `ffmpeg`'s input seeking;
02:28:49 out   the picture is accurate to about a frame, not sample-accurate.
02:28:49 out   - Video is capped at 1280x720 and 30 fps to keep decode affordable. The cap can
02:28:49 out   be raised in `src/player.rs` (and in `mechanics.json`).
02:28:49 out   - The dark/light choice, accent colour and Keep on top switch are stored in
02:28:49 out   `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vidsync/settings.json` (usually under `.config`) and restored
02:28:49 out   on the next launch. Keep on top uses the X11 `_NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE` protocol;
02:28:49 out   a Wayland compositor that does not expose that protocol may ignore it.
02:28:49 out   - The AppImage is built on x86_64; other architectures need their own static
02:28:49 out   ffmpeg and appimagetool build.
02:28:49 out   ## Licence
02:28:49 out   VIDSYNC is MIT licensed; see `LICENSE`. The bundled DejaVu fonts remain under the
02:28:49 out   Bitstream Vera licence in `assets/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt`.
